Handling log entries with varying formats can be challenging, but Grok patterns provide a flexible way to parse diverse log structures. Here are some strategies to handle varying log formats effectively:

### 1. Use Multiple Grok Patterns

You can define multiple Grok patterns and apply them sequentially until one matches the log entry. This approach allows you to handle different formats within the same log stream.

### Example Configuration

```ruby
filter {
  grok {
    match => { "message" => [
      "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] Agent %{INT:agent_id1} interacted with Agent %{INT:agent_id2}: Action=%{WORD:action}, Result=%{WORD:result}",
      "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] Agent %{INT:agent_id}: %{WORD:action} %{WORD:result}",
      "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] Agent %{INT:agent_id1} interacted with Agent %{INT:agent_id2}: Action=%{WORD:action}, Result=%{WORD:result}, Duration=%{INT:duration}ms"
    ] }
  }
}
```

### 2. Use Conditionals to Apply Different Patterns

You can use conditionals to apply different Grok patterns based on specific conditions, such as the presence of certain keywords or patterns in the log entry.

### Example Configuration

```ruby
filter {
  if [message] =~ /interacted with/ {
    grok {
      match => { "message" => "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] Agent %{INT:agent_id1} interacted with Agent %{INT:agent_id2}: Action=%{WORD:action}, Result=%{WORD:result}" }
    }
  } else if [message] =~ /Action=/ {
    grok {
      match => { "message" => "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] Agent %{INT:agent_id}: %{WORD:action} %{WORD:result}" }
    }
  } else if [message] =~ /Duration=/ {
    grok {
      match => { "message" => "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] Agent %{INT:agent_id1} interacted with Agent %{INT:agent_id2}: Action=%{WORD:action}, Result=%{WORD:result}, Duration=%{INT:duration}ms" }
    }
  }
}
```

### 3. Use Named Capture Groups for Flexibility

You can use named capture groups to make your Grok patterns more flexible and reusable. This approach allows you to define common patterns and reuse them across different log formats.

### Example Configuration

```ruby
filter {
  grok {
    match => { "message" => [
      "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] %{AGENT_INTERACTION_PATTERN}",
      "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] %{AGENT_ACTION_PATTERN}"
    ] }
  }
}

filter {
  grok {
    match => { "AGENT_INTERACTION_PATTERN" => "Agent %{INT:agent_id1} interacted with Agent %{INT:agent_id2}: Action=%{WORD:action}, Result=%{WORD:result}" }
  }
}

filter {
  grok {
    match => { "AGENT_ACTION_PATTERN" => "Agent %{INT:agent_id}: %{WORD:action} %{WORD:result}" }
  }
}
```

### 4. Use Conditional Filters to Handle Missing Fields

Sometimes, log entries may be missing certain fields. You can use conditional filters to handle these cases gracefully.

### Example Configuration

```ruby
filter {
  grok {
    match => { "message" => "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] Agent %{INT:agent_id1} interacted with Agent %{INT:agent_id2}: Action=%{WORD:action}, Result=%{WORD:result}" }
  }
  if !("_grokparsefailure" in [tags]) {
    mutate {
      remove_tag => ["_grokparsefailure"]
    }
  }
}
```

### 5. Use Custom Grok Patterns for Complex Formats

For more complex log formats, you can define custom Grok patterns to handle specific structures.

### Example Custom Grok Pattern

```ruby
filter {
  grok {
    match => { "message" => "%{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:timestamp} \[%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel}\] %{AGENT_INTERACTION_PATTERN}" }
  }
}

filter {
